---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { housingExtract, cpiExtract } from '../lib/geo-links';
import foodFile from '../data/statcan/food-prices.json';
import { formatRefMonth } from '../lib/utils';
import { sourcesJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const jsonLd = sourcesJsonLd();
---

<Layout
  title="Data sources and methodology"
  description="How Canadian Data Insights sources Statistics Canada census profiles, housing tables, the Consumer Price Index, and retail prices under the Open Government Licence - Canada."
  canonicalPath="/sources/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="bg-background py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="font-heading text-4xl font-bold text-primary sm:text-5xl">
          Data <span class="text-tertiary">sources</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-sans text-lg leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
          Where our numbers come from, how we process them, and how we attribute Statistics
          Canada data.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-8 font-sans leading-relaxed text-on-surface">
        <div>
          <h2 class="mb-4 font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-primary">Primary source</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Canadian Data Insights is built from the official{' '}
            <strong>Statistics Canada Census of Population, 2021</strong>. We do not invent
            estimates or mix in third-party extrapolations for the demographic figures shown
            on location profiles.
          </p>
          <p>
            Profiles cover provinces, territories, and municipalities with a population of at
            least 5,000, drawn from census geography identifiers and published characteristic
            tables.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="mb-4 font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-primary">What we process</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Raw census extracts are cleaned and reshaped into location-level profiles. For
            each place we surface population and growth, households, income, languages,
            education, occupation, industry, place of work, dwelling types, and related
            rankings within the same geography level. Education, occupation, industry, and
            place of work from that file are shown on location profiles and in the comparison
            tool. Shares on those charts are calculated here from the published counts.
          </p>
          <p>
            Charts, comparisons, and rankings on this site are derived from those processed
            tables. Presentation choices (layout, labels, rankings) are ours; the underlying
            counts and rates remain Statistics Canada's published census data.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="mb-4 font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-primary">Housing tenure and shelter costs</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Tenure, the share of households spending 30% or more of income on shelter, and monthly
            shelter costs come from the 2021 Census tables below. The extract was taken on{' '}
            {housingExtract.retrieved}. Values are copied from the published tables for all private
            households, not an income or family subgroup. Suppressed cells are left blank. We do not
            estimate a municipality that is outside the table's geography.
          </p>
          <ul class="mb-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
            {
              housingExtract.tables.map((table) => (
                <li>
                  <a
                    href={table.url}
                    class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-tertiary"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >
                    Table {table.id}
                  </a>
                  . {table.title}.
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
          <p>
            Those tables cover Canada, the provinces and territories, census metropolitan areas, and
            census agglomerations. A municipality page shows them only when its name matches one of
            those published areas, and the page names that area. Owner and renter shares, and the
            share spending 30% or more of income on shelter, are calculated on this site from the
            published household counts. Shelter-cost bands can differ slightly from the household
            total because census counts are randomly rounded.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="mb-4 font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-primary">Consumer prices</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            The <a href="/cost-of-living/" class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-tertiary">cost-of-living page</a>{' '}
            uses two monthly tables. The all-items index on this build is {formatRefMonth(cpiExtract.referenceMonth)},
            compared with {formatRefMonth(cpiExtract.comparisonMonth)}. The 12-month percent change is
            calculated here from those two published index levels. It is not a separate Statistics
            Canada “percent change” series. Grocery prices use each product's latest published month.
            The extract was taken on {cpiExtract.retrieved}.
          </p>
          <ul class="mb-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>
              <a
                href={cpiExtract.table.url}
                class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-tertiary"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Table {cpiExtract.table.id}
              </a>
              . {cpiExtract.table.title}. Index base {cpiExtract.table.uom}.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a
                href={foodFile.table.url}
                class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-tertiary"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Table {foodFile.table.id}
              </a>
              . {foodFile.table.title}.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            City CPI rows follow Statistics Canada's price-index geography, which is not the
            municipality alone. The food index is published for Canada, the provinces, Whitehorse,
            and Yellowknife. A location page shows a price card only when one of these geographies
            matches that place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="mb-4 font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-primary">
            Open Government Licence - Canada
          </h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Statistics Canada data is used under the{' '}
            <a
              href="https://open.canada.ca/en/open-government-licence-canada"
              class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-tertiary"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Open Government Licence - Canada
            </a>
            . That licence lets anyone copy, modify, publish, translate, adapt, and distribute
            the information, provided Statistics Canada is acknowledged as the source and the
            licence terms are followed.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">Suggested attribution for reuse of figures shown here:</p>
          <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-primary/20 pl-4 italic text-on-surface-variant">
            Adapted from Statistics Canada, Census of Population, 2021. This does not
            constitute an endorsement by Statistics Canada of this product.
          </blockquote>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="mb-4 font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-primary">
            Accuracy and limitations
          </h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Census values can be affected by suppression, rounding, and geography definitions.
            We aim for faithful parsing and clear visualization, but we do not warrant
            absolute completeness for every derived chart or ranking.
          </p>
          <p>
            For official definitions, methodology notes, and downloads, visit{' '}
            <a
              href="https://www.statcan.gc.ca/"
              class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-tertiary"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Statistics Canada
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="mb-4 font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-primary">Related policies</h2>
          <p>
            See our{' '}
            <a
              href="/privacy/"
              class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-tertiary"
            >
              Privacy Policy
            </a>{' '}
            and{' '}
            <a
              href="/terms/"
              class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-tertiary"
            >
              Terms of Service
            </a>{' '}
            for how we handle site analytics and use of this platform.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
